    {
      "id": "s05",
      "type": "APPLE_STAGE",
      "transition": "push",
      "background": "zoneB",
      "narration": "That thinness claim needs care, because it's true in one state only. Open, the Duo is five point two millimetres, beating the iPhone Air at five point six. Closed, you hold two of those plus a hinge, so it's eleven point three. Both numbers are honest; one gets advertised.",
      "data": {
        "appleStage": {
          "headline": "Thinnest iPhone ever, [when open]",
          "kind": "compare-bars",
          "caption": "Depth, in millimetres",
          "premise": "The claim is true open and false closed, so both states belong on screen.",
          "color": "blue",
          "cells": [
            {
              "label": "iPhone Duo, open",
              "text": "bar",
              "sub": "5.2 mm",
              "value": 5.2,
              "atWord": 11.833
            },
            {
              "label": "iPhone Air",
              "text": "bar",
              "sub": "5.6 mm",
              "value": 5.6,
              "atWord": 20.583
            },
            {
              "label": "iPhone Duo, closed",
              "text": "bar",
              "sub": "11.3 mm",
              "value": 11.3,
              "atWord": 25.833
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      "durationFrames": 521,
      "audio": "audio/duo_long_s05.mp3",
      "timingSource": "tts"
    },
    {
      "id": "s06",
      "type": "APPLE_STAGE",
      "transition": "fade",
      "background": "zoneB",
      "narration": "Two screens, doing different jobs. Outside there's a five point four inch panel carrying ninety percent of an iPhone 18 Pro screen area, so the closed phone is a whole phone rather than a preview window. Open it and you get seven point six inches with a nano texture finish.",
      "data": {
        "appleStage": {
          "headline": "A whole phone, [then] a small tablet",
          "kind": "duo-pair",
          "token": "open",
          "caption": "5.4-inch outside, 7.6-inch inside",
          "premise": "The outer panel carries 90% of an iPhone 18 Pro screen area. The inner one is nano-texture.",
          "color": "green",
          "cells": [
            {
              "label": "Outer display",
              "text": "outer",
              "sub": "5.4-inch, 1398 x 2034",
              "atWord": 7
            },
            {
              "label": "Inner display",
              "text": "inner",
              "sub": "7.6-inch, nano-texture",
              "atWord": 21.667
            },
            {
              "label": "Under the screen",
              "text": "selfie",
              "sub": "no notch, no cutout",
              "atWord": 1.667
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      "durationFrames": 495,
      "audio": "audio/duo_long_s06.mp3",
      "timingSource": "tts"
    },
    {
      "id": "s07",
      "type": "APPLE_STAGE",
      "transition": "iris",
      "background": "zoneC",
      "narration": "Here's the part I opened with. On that inner screen the FaceTime camera sits underneath the display, so there’s no notch, no punch hole and no island breaking the surface, because the camera only appears when it’s needed. Every rival foldable puts a hole in its big screen.",
      "data": {
        "appleStage": {
          "headline": "The camera is [under] the screen",
          "kind": "duo-pair",
          "token": "open",
          "caption": "It appears only when needed",
          "premise": "No notch and no cutout in the inner display: the camera sits beneath the surface.",
          "color": "purple",
          "cells": [
            {
              "label": "Under-display camera",
              "text": "selfie",
              "sub": "appears only when needed",
              "atWord": 10.833
            },
            {
              "label": "No cutout",
              "text": "inner",
              "sub": "an uninterrupted panel",
              "atWord": 19.5
            },
            {
              "label": "Outer Center Stage",
              "text": "outer",
              "sub": "12MP, on the cover",
              "atWord": 25
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      "durationFrames": 478,
      "audio": "audio/duo_long_s07.mp3",
      "timingSource": "tts"
    },
    {
      "id": "s08",
      "type": "APPLE_STAGE",
      "transition": "slide",
      "background": "zoneA",
      "narration": "All that width has to earn itself, and iOS 27 is where it does. Unfold the Duo and the system adapts as it opens, so you can run two apps side by side, or hold two home screen pages at once. Apps notice the fold happening, which is why the handoff feels continuous rather than like a relaunch.",
      "data": {
        "appleStage": {
          "headline": "Two apps, [side by side]",
          "kind": "duo-pair",
          "token": "open",
          "caption": "iOS 27 adapts as it opens",
          "premise": "The inner panel is close to 4:3, which is what makes two full-height apps practical.",
          "color": "green",
          "cells": [
            {
              "label": "Two apps at once",
              "text": "inner",
              "sub": "side by side",
              "atWord": 20.917
            },
            {
              "label": "Two home pages",
              "text": "outer",
              "sub": "or one big grid",
              "atWord": 26.333
            },
            {
              "label": "Apps see the fold",
              "text": "hinge",
              "sub": "continuous, not relaunched",
              "atWord": 28.75
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      "durationFrames": 545,
      "audio": "audio/duo_long_s08.mp3",
      "timingSource": "tts"
    },
    {
      "id": "s09",
      "type": "RECORDED_STEP",
      "transition": "letterbox",
      "background": "zoneC",
      "narration": "Round the back, a pair rather than the Pro trio: a forty eight megapixel Fusion main and a forty eight megapixel ultra wide, in a pill shaped housing with the flash beside them. Two lenses rather than three is a choice the hinge forces, because a telephoto needs depth and depth is the one dimension a folding phone spends twice. What that costs you is optical reach, which is the next thing worth looking at.",
